1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:04,877
I'm [Name];
I led exploration, labels and bias.

2
00:00:04,877 --> 00:00:09,603
First, a fallacy check:
we scanned 224 subgroup comparisons

3
00:00:09,603 --> 00:00:12,236
for Simpson's paradox,
and six reversed sign.

4
00:00:13,486 --> 00:00:17,629
A causal diagram suggests why:
BMI lies between race and diabetes,

5
00:00:17,629 --> 00:00:20,556
so adjusting
for it distorts the comparison.

6
00:00:20,556 --> 00:00:22,900
We report this as exploratory.

7
00:00:23,300 --> 00:00:25,615
Research question one: the label.

8
00:00:25,615 --> 00:00:28,994
Our label combines two sources,
and they agree substantially

9
00:00:28,994 --> 00:00:32,673
but far from perfectly:
Cohen's kappa is 0.70.

10
00:00:33,923 --> 00:00:40,077
Of 819 self-reported diabetics,
250 test below 6.5 percent,

11
00:00:40,077 --> 00:00:42,447
and two thirds of those
take insulin or pills.

12
00:00:43,497 --> 00:00:46,271
Treatment lowers
the very marker we label by,

13
00:00:46,271 --> 00:00:49,900
so a blood-test-only label
would call treated patients healthy.

14
00:00:50,300 --> 00:00:51,750
The other direction:

15
00:00:51,750 --> 00:00:57,073
152 adults have HbA1c of 6.5 or more
but no diagnosis.

16
00:00:58,323 --> 00:01:00,580
Among adults with high HbA1c,

17
00:01:00,580 --> 00:01:05,137
the uninsured are 23.2 percentage points
more often undiagnosed,

18
00:01:05,137 --> 00:01:09,652
interval 6.2 to 40.1,
an association from a small subgroup.

19
00:01:10,902 --> 00:01:13,602
So the label also records access to care.

20
00:01:13,602 --> 00:01:17,159
That is why we also evaluate
on adults not yet diagnosed:

21
00:01:17,159 --> 00:01:18,900
the people screening is for.

22
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:23,279
The leakage question,
from [Name]'s experiments.

23
00:01:23,279 --> 00:01:26,652
Naive preparation scores an AUC of 0.961.

24
00:01:26,652 --> 00:01:30,482
Minimal preparation removes
only the post-diagnosis items:

25
00:01:30,482 --> 00:01:33,633
treatment, diagnosis history
and prior glucose testing.

26
00:01:33,633 --> 00:01:38,841
Same model, same folds: the AUC falls
to 0.828, a paired drop of 0.131.

27
00:01:40,591 --> 00:01:42,563
A deterioration we wanted:

28
00:01:42,563 --> 00:01:45,900
the 0.961 was the label leaking back in,
not skill.

29
00:01:46,300 --> 00:01:49,873
[Name]'s shallow decision tree
shows the mechanism.

30
00:01:49,873 --> 00:01:54,966
Its root is a treatment item; one branch
then splits on age at diagnosis.

31
00:01:54,966 --> 00:01:57,895
But 86 percent were never asked

32
00:01:57,895 --> 00:02:02,833
that question, and median imputation
filled every blank with 50.

33
00:02:04,083 --> 00:02:07,900
So 'age 50' became a code
for 'never diagnosed'.

34
00:02:08,300 --> 00:02:09,853
Most unexpected for me:

35
00:02:09,853 --> 00:02:14,626
at one shared threshold, the model flags
70 of every 100 diagnosed diabetics,

36
00:02:14,626 --> 00:02:17,818
but only 52 of 100 undiagnosed ones.

37
00:02:19,068 --> 00:02:20,900
[Name] takes the last question.
